From: Vane maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] android: report CertPathValidatorException by reason instead of
 assuming revocation

Android's PKIXRevocationChecker throws CertPathValidatorException for
undeterminable revocation status, not only for a definitive revocation, and
verifyCertificateChain maps every such exception to StatusCode.Revoked. The
existing escape hatch for this is guarded by BuildConfig.TEST, so released
artifacts still report Revoked and callers cannot reach a CRL-only host.

Let's Encrypt retired OCSP in August 2025 and Google Trust Services followed,
so certificates with a CRL distribution point and no OCSP responder URL are
ordinary and a growing share of the web. For those, Android throws
"Certificate does not specify OCSP responder" with BasicReason.UNSPECIFIED.
SOFT_FAIL does not cover it — that option forgives network errors and
tryLater/internalError responder answers, not a missing responder.

Reporting undeterminable status as revoked deters no attacker. An attacker
holding a genuinely revoked certificate reaches the same branch simply by
omitting the staple, and nothing here enforces Must-Staple. Separately,
network-fetched revocation is already inoperative on modern Android: OCSP
responder URLs are cleartext http:// per RFC 6960 and usesCleartextTraffic
defaults to false for targetSdk 28+, so the fetch fails and SOFT_FAIL forgives
it. Measured on an API 35 emulator:

    cleartextPermittedGlobally=false
    cleartextPermitted(ocsps.ssl.com)=false
    GET http://ocsps.ssl.com/ -> IOException: Cleartext HTTP traffic to
      ocsps.ssl.com not permitted

and a genuinely revoked host (revoked-rsa-dv.ssl.com, CA still serving OCSP)
connects successfully both before and after this change, with an entry log in
the catch block never firing — the exception is not thrown at all.

So the strict mapping costs availability and buys nothing. A stapled response
that says revoked still reports REVOKED and is still refused, which is the
case that actually works today and is preserved here.

This supersedes the BuildConfig.TEST workaround and closes #221. It is
narrower than #179 and independent of it: #179 can still add fetch-capable
revocation later without conflicting.

Closes: #221

--- a/android/rustls-platform-verifier/src/main/java/org/rustls/platformverifier/CertificateVerifier.kt
+++ b/android/rustls-platform-verifier/src/main/java/org/rustls/platformverifier/CertificateVerifier.kt
@@
             } catch (e: CertPathValidatorException) {
-                // LetsEncrypt no longer include OCSP information (as OCSP is being deprecated) which Android is not
-                // happy with since it *only* tries OCSP by default. We aren't 100% decided on how to fix this yet for real
-                // (see https://github.com/rustls/rustls-platform-verifier/pull/179) so for now we implement an out for
-                // tests to allow regular maintenance to proceed.
-                if (BuildConfig.TEST && e.reason == CertPathValidatorException.BasicReason.UNSPECIFIED) {
-                    return VerificationResult(StatusCode.Ok)
-                }
-
-                return VerificationResult(StatusCode.Revoked, e.toString())
+                // Only a definitive "this certificate was revoked" verdict may
+                // block the connection. Android lands in this catch for
+                // undeterminable status too — above all `UNSPECIFIED`, which is
+                // what it throws ("Certificate does not specify OCSP responder")
+                // for an end entity carrying no OCSP responder URL. SOFT_FAIL
+                // does not cover that: it forgives network errors and
+                // tryLater/internalError, not a missing responder.
+                //
+                // A stapled response that says "revoked" still reports REVOKED
+                // and is still refused, which is the case that actually matters.
+                if (e.reason == CertPathValidatorException.BasicReason.REVOKED) {
+                    return VerificationResult(StatusCode.Revoked, e.toString())
+                }
+
+                Log.w(TAG, "revocation status undeterminable (${e.reason}), allowing: $e")
             }

Notes for the reviewer, not part of the commit message:

- No API change: StatusCode values, the verifyCertificateChain signature and
  VerificationResult fields are untouched, so src/verification/android.rs needs
  no corresponding change.
- Falling out of the catch reaches the existing `return
  VerificationResult(StatusCode.Ok)` at the end of the method, which is the
  same path taken when revocation checking is skipped on old API levels.
- Verified downstream on an API 35 arm64 emulator: www.cloudflare.com and
  badssl.com (both CRL-only) go from Revoked to HTTP 200; example.com, which
  still staples, continues to work; a self-signed certificate is still
  rejected by pass 1.
